(15,'Providencia stuartii','Facultative anaerobic Gram-negative mobile rod. Normal part of GI flora and ubiquitous in the environment. A cause of cUTIs, often nosocomial or catheter-related. P. Stuartii may convert tryptophan in the urine into purple compounds (“purple bag syndrome”).\nCommonly multi-drug resistant; risk increases with age, hospitalizations, instrumentation use, and antibiotic therapy.\nIntrinsically resistant to Polymyxins (Colistin.), Tigecycline, and Nitrofurantoin.\nPLEASE NOTE: Additional mechanisms of antibiotic resistance (efflux pumps, decreased membrane permeability, etc.) may confer intrinsic ABX resistance even in the absence of the regular markers (negative PCR results).\n','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCefixime.\nTMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nCefiderocol.\n\n',0,'NULL','','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCefixime.\nTMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nCefiderocol.\n','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCefixime.\nTMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nCefiderocol.\n','TMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCefiderocol.\n','TMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCefiderocol.\n','TMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCefiderocol.\n','TMP-SMX. \nFosfomycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefepime.\nPlazomicin.\nMeropenem.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nCefiderocol.\n','TM