(16,'Pseudomonas aeruginosa','Gram-negative non-fermenting, motile bacillus. Ubiquitous in the environment; Usually a nosocomial, opportunistic pathogen, common in complicated and catheter or instrumentation-related UTIs.\nMDR common; risk increases with age, hospitalizations, instrumentation use, and antibiotic therapy.\nResistance mechanisms: constitutive or plasmid beta-lactamases, plasmid-mediated ESBLs (TEM, SHV, CTX-M), production of class B metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM, VIM, IMP).\nIntrinsically resistant to Nitrofurantoin.\nAcquired polymyxin (Colistin, Polymyxin B) resistance is common.\nPlease Note: Intrinsic FosA gene requires an increased dose of Fosfomycin.  \nPlease Note: Carbapenem resistance is usually due to impaired permeability or efflux pumps, not a carbapenemase (carbapenem resistance is common without expressing the regular markers).','Ciprofloxacin, Levofloxacin. \nCefepime, Ceftazidime.\nPlazomicin, Tobramycin.\nFosfomycin.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\nCefiderocol.',0,'NULL','','Ciprofloxacin, Levofloxacin. \nCefepime.\nCeftazidime.\nPlazomicin, Tobramycin.\nFosfomycin.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\nCefiderocol.','Ciprofloxacin, Levofloxacin. \nCefepime.\nCeftazidime.\nPlazomicin, Tobramycin.\nFosfomycin.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\nCefiderocol.','Ciprofloxacin, Levofloxacin. \nCefepime.\nPlazomicin, Tobramycin.\nFosfomycin.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\nCefiderocol.','Ciprofloxacin, Levofloxacin. \nCefepime.\nPlazomicin, Tobramycin.\nFosfomycin.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin.\nCeftazidime-Avibactam.\nCeft