(31,'Streptococcus pyogenes','Gram-positive, Group A Streptococcus. Found in the human throat and on the skin, it commonly causes strep throat, impetigo, and scarlet fever. UTI infections are rare and occur in compromised patients with systemic or local conditions predisposing them to urinary tract infections.\nIt may be resistant to Macrolides, Tetracyclines, and Clindamycin.\n','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.',1,'NULL','','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','','','Ceftaroline.\nTMP-SMX.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\n','Ceftaroline.\nTMP-SMX.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\n','Ampicillin, Amoxicillin.\nDoxycycline.\nFosfomycin.\nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nDaptomycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nLinezolid.','Ampicillin, Amoxicillin.\nDoxycycline.\nFosfomycin.\nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nDaptomycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nLinezolid.','Ampicillin, Amoxicillin.\nDoxycycline.\nFosfomycin.\nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nDaptomycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nLinezolid.','Ampicillin, Amoxicillin.\nDoxycycline.\nFosfomycin.\nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nDaptomycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nLinezolid.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Penicillin G. \nAmoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nCephalexin.\nTMP-SMX.\nLevofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Cefepime, Ceftaroline.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nLinezolid.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','',''),
(32,'Bacteroides fragilis','Gram-negative anaerobic bacillus. A significant component of the intestinal flora. It can cause infection of surrounding tissue following surgery or trauma. Urinary tract infection occurs mainly in the presence of obstruction. \n','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\n',0,'NULL','','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\n','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\n','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\n','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\n','',''),
(33,'Prevotella bivia','Gram-negative anaerobic bacillus. Usually recovered in anaerobic cultures. A normal part of oral, upper respiratory, intestinal and female genital tract flora. Implicated in opportunistic, multimicrobial infections, including in the GU tract. Prevotella species are intrinsically resistant to aminoglycosides, aztreonam, trimethoprim, sulfonamides and fosfomycin. Also, high rates of resistance to penicillins, cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones and clindamycin.','Amoxicillin-Clavulanate.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem.\nCeftolozane-Tazobactam.\nMeropenem-Vaborbactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin-Relebactam.\n',0,'','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','',''),
(34,'Mycoplasma genitalium','Aerobic, fastidious organism lacks a cell wall. Colonizes male/female GU tract. Responsible for up to 30% of non-gonococcal urethritis cases in men, less common in women.\nIntrinsically resistant to Beta-Lactams and other agents that target the cell wall.','Azithromycin.\nDoxycycline.\nMoxifloxacin.',0,'NULL','','Azithromycin.\nMoxifloxacin.','Azithromycin.\nMoxifloxacin.','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Azithromycin.\nMoxifloxacin.','Azithromycin.\nMoxifloxacin.','','','','','','','Doxycycline.\nMoxifloxacin.','Doxycycline.\nMoxifloxacin.','Doxycycline.\nMoxifloxacin.','Doxycycline.\nMoxifloxacin.','',''),
(35,'Mycoplasma hominis','Aerobic, lacks a cell wall. A normal component of male/female genital flora. Mainly described as causing disease in women during pregnancy or immunocompromised patients.\nIntrinsically resistant to Beta-Lactams and Macrolides.\nPLEASE NOTE: Pathogenicity unclear, may be a contaminant. Treat if symptomatic. If more pathogenic bacteria are present, treatment choices should prioritize them. \n','Doxycycline.\nClindamycin.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.\n\n',0,'NULL','0','Clindamycin.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.\n\n','Clindamycin.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.\n\n','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Clindamycin.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.\n\n','Clindamycin.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.\n\n','','','','','','','Doxycycline.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.','Doxycycline.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.','Doxycycline.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.','Doxycycline.\nOfloxacin\nLevofloxacin.\nLinezolid.','0','0'),
(36,'Ureaplasma urealyticum','Aerobic, lacks a cell wall. A normal component of male/female genital flora. Role in disease is unclear; possible associations include urethritis, chorioamnionitis, pregnancy complications, and pneumonia (neonatal).\nIntrinsically resistant to Beta-Lactams and other agents that target the cell wall.\nPLEASE NOTE: Pathogenicity unclear, may be a contaminant. Treat if symptomatic. If more pathogenic bacteria are present, treatment choices should prioritize them. \n','Doxycycline.\nAzithromycin.\nClindamycin.\nStreptomycin.\nOfloxacin.',0,'NULL','0','Azithromycin.\nClindamycin.\nStreptomycin.\nOfloxacin.','Azithromycin.\nClindamycin.\nStreptomycin.\nOfloxacin.','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Azithromycin.\nClindamycin.\nStreptomycin.\nOfloxacin.','Azithromycin.\nClindamycin.\nStreptomycin.\nOfloxacin.','','','','','','','','','','','0','0');
INSERT INTO `ci_bacteria_gi` VALUES
(1,'Adenovirus (41)','Common cause of diarrhea; a preceding or concurrent respiratory illness is common. Duration can be prolonged (1-2 weeks). Easily transmitted, persists in the environment.  \n\nTreatment is hydration; there is no effective anti-viral therapy.','N/A',0,'','','','','','','','','','',''),
(2,'Norovirus (I, II)','The most common cause of acute gastroenteritis, non-bacterial diarrhea, and epidemic gastroenteritis in restaurants, schools, healthcare settings, etc; Symptoms include sudden onset of nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, and non-bloody diarrhea. Extremely contagious. Peaks in winter. Duration is 12-60 hours, with rapid recovery within 72 hours except for immunocompromised hosts.\n \nTreatment is hydration; there is no effective anti-viral therapy.','N/A',0,'','','','','','','','','','',''),
(3,'Rotavirus','Leading cause of diarrhea-related illness  in children younger than 5. Peak season – winter. Symptoms include vomiting and non-bloody diarrhea that lasts 1 to 5 days. Adult infections are mostly asymptomatic.\n\nTreatment is hydration; there is no effective anti-viral therapy.','N/A',0,'','','','','','','','','','',''),
(4,'Giardia lamblia','Also known as Giardia Duodenalis and Giardia Intestinalis,  it is a highly contagious flagellated protozoan that contaminates food and water.  The most common intestinal parasite in North America, can cause either a slow-onset acute diarrhea or a chronic form with abdominal pain, loose stools, bloating, and malabsorption with weight loss.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.',0,'','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.','Tinidazole.\nNitazoxanide.\nMetronidazole.\nParomomycin.'),
(5,'Campylobacter coli','Gram-negative bacteria. In the U.S., a top cause of food-borne acute illness with diarrhea.  Campylobacter spp. is responsible for a significant proportion of culture-negative gastroenteritis. Infections acquired from contaminated meat or poultry (most cases), water, milk products, pets, pets, petting zoos and farms. Cause of traveller’s diarrhea, especially in SE Asia. Antibiotics usually not needed in normal hosts.\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n\n',0,'','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n\n'),
(6,'Campylobacter jejuni','Gram-negative bacteria. In the U.S., a top cause of food-borne acute illness with diarrhea.  Campylobacter spp. is responsible for a significant proportion of culture-negative gastroenteritis. Infections acquired from contaminated meat or poultry (most cases), water, milk products, pets, pets, petting zoos and farms. Cause of traveller’s diarrhea, especially in SE Asia. ','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.\n\n',0,'','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClyndamycin.\nDoxycycline.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nTigecycline.\n\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.\n\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.\n\n','Azytromycin.\nErythromycin.\nClindamycin.\nCiprofloxacin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin.\nMeropenem.\nGentamicin.\nTigecycline.\n\n'),
(7,'E. Coli (VTEC)','Verotoxin producing E. coli.  Also known as Shiga toxin producing E. coli (STEC) and Enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC). Causes bloody diarrheal illness, along with fever and elevated WBC. Shiga toxin causes hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS): hemolytic anemia, renal injury, thrombocytopenia. HUS complications include CNS damage, pancreatitis and other manifestations. HUS has a 10% mortality rate; 50% of patients have some degree of permanent renal and/or CNS damage.\n\nHighly contagious, it is spread through contaminated meat (beef, lamb) or from person to person. Primary care regimen is hydration. Avoid anti-peristaltic drugs. Look for evidence of HUS: low platelet count, renal injury, CNS signs and symptoms. Notify public health authorities promptly.','Azithromycin.',0,'','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.','No option.','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.','Azithromycin.'),
(8,'Salmonella sp.','Aerobic, gram negative, non-typhoidal Salmonella spp. cause enterocolitis and are a major cause of food-borne infection, primarily from poultry and eggs. Diarrhea, bloody stools (35%) nausea, vomiting, fever (>70%), abdominal cramping, constitutional symptoms. At highest risk for severe disease are children under age 5, immunosuppressed patients and the elderly.\n\nFor moderate disease the main treatment is hydration. For severe disease (>8 stools/day), administer IV fluid and antibiotics.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nCiprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.',0,'','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\n','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\n','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nCiprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nMeropenem.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nCiprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nCiprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nCiprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.','Ceftriaxone.\nCefotaxime.\nCiprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAzithromycin.\nMeropenem.'),
(9,'Yersinia enterocolitica','Facultative Gram negative bacilli.  Transmitted by the fecal-oral route, contaminated food (unpasteurized milk, undercooked pork, shellfish), water, blood or direct contact with carrier animals (pigs, cattle, sheep, chicken, dogs, cats, and birds).\n\nCommon clinical presentations include acute diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea & vomiting. No treatment recommended except for severe disease.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.',0,'','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','TMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nDoxycycline.','Ciprofloxacin.\nLevofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nDoxycycline.'),
(10,'Clostridioides difficile','Gram positive anaerobic bacilli, found in feces (human, animal), water and soil. Produces toxins A and B that cause colitis in humans. Risk factors include advanced age, hospitalization, prior or concomitant systemic antibacterial therapy (fluoroquinolone, carbapenems, cephalosporins, clindamycin), cancer chemotherapy, gastrointestinal surgery. Symptoms include acute or chronic diarrhea and cramps, with or without fever, elevated fecal WBCs, positive fecal lactoferrin, increased serum creatinine.\n\n','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.',1,'','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Metronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nVancomycin.\nMetronidazole.','Fidaxomicin.\nMetronidazole.'),
(11,'Coagulase-negative staphylococci','Gram-positive aerobic coccus, occurring in clusters. Predominantly found on the skin and mucous membranes, have a propensity to produce biofilm, allowing for adherence to medical devices. They are increasingly recognized as agents of clinically significant infection, including bacteremia and endocarditis. A primary source of sepsis in neonates. Resistance to methicillin is common.','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\nOxacillin.\n',1,'','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\n','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\n','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\n','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\n','Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\n','Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\n','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\nOxacillin.\n','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nOxacillin.\n','Vancomycin.\nGentamicin.\nOxacillin.\n','Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.\nOxacillin.\n'),
(12,'Enterococcus faecalis','Facultatively anaerobic Gram-positive bacteria, normally found in colonic flora. Increased concentrations associated with chronic inflammation of the colon.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ',1,'','Vancomycin.\nTigecycline.\n','Vancomycin.\nTigecycline.\n','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Vancomycin.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. '),
(13,'Listeria monocytogenes','Gram positive bacilli. Recognized as a cause of food-associated febrile gastroenteritis. Found in dairy products (milk, soft cheeses), sea food, deli, and produce. Value of oral antibiotics is unknown and infection is self-limited. Frequently seen with fecal carriage/shedding without causing illness.\n\nABX treatment might be reasonable in populations at increased risk of severe systemic disease: pregnant women, neonates, the elderly, and immunocompromised hosts ','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.',1,'','Ampicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.','Ampicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.','Ampicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.','Ampicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.','TMP-SMX.','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin.\n','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin.\n','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin.\nTMP-SMX.'),
(14,'Streptococcus agalactiae','Group B strep. (GBS), an important cause of bacteremia and peripartum infections.\n\n\nFound in GI/GU tracts. More common in adults >65 and those w/ co-morbidities. In the United States, GBS bacteria are a leading cause of meningitis and bloodstream infections in a newborn\'s first three months of life. Newborns are at increased risk for GBS disease if their mother tests positive for the bacteria late in pregnancy.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nClindamycin.',1,'','Vancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Vancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Vancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Vancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\n','Vancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nVancomycin.\nClindamycin.','Penicillin G.\nAmpicillin.\nClindamycin.');
INSERT INTO `ci_bacteria_wound` VALUES
(1,'Acinetobacter baumannii','Gram-negative, aerobic, pleomorphic and non-motile. Opportunistic bacterial pathogen primarily associated with hospital-acquired infections in immunocompromised individuals.  A common cause of wounds, burn wounds and surgical site infections, including necrotizing fasciitis. It has a high rate of antibiotic resistance, with about 50% of isolates being multi-drug resistant. ','Cefepime, Ceftazidime.\nAmpicillin-sulbactam.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nTigecycline.',0,'','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.\nCefepime.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\n','Polymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\n','Polymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Polymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Polymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Polymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.','','','','','','','','','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.','Colistin + Imipenem-cilastatin.\nColistin + Meropenem.\nColistin + Ampicillin-sublactam.\nTigecycline.','',''),
(2,'Citrobacter freundii','Enteric aerobic gram-negative bacilli. Found in soil, water, food and gut flora of humans and animals. Associated with superficial and deep infections of the skin and soft tissue and post-op wound infections in both normal and immuno-compromised hosts. May express ampC (inducible), ESBLs, KPCs, or NDM-1. Often concomitant resistance to fluoroquinolones, aminoglycosides and TMP/SMX.\n','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin, Moxifloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCefepime.',0,'NULL','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\n','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\n','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\n','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem. \nAmpicillin-sulbactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem. \nAmpicillin-sulbactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem. \nAmpicillin-sulbactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem. \nAmpicillin-sulbactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.','','','','','','','','','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem. \nTigecycline.','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem. \nTigecycline.','0','0'),
(3,'Enterobacter aerogenes','Gram-negative, aerobic facultative, bacilli.  Found in water, sewage, and soil. Common cause of nosocomial infections including burn, surgical wound and device-related infections. Inherently resistant to ampicillin, first-generation cephalosporins and macrolides. High levels of drug resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCefepime.',0,'','Ciprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nMeropenem-Varbobactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\r\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\r\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','','','','','','','','','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCefepime.','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCefepime.','',''),
(4,'Enterobacter cloacae','Gram-negative, aerobic facultative, bacilli.  Found in water, sewage, and soil. Common cause of nosocomial infections including burn, surgical wound and device-related infections. Inherently resistant to ampicillin, first-generation cephalosporins and macrolides. High levels of drug resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCefepime.',0,'NULL','Ciprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\r\nTMP-SMX.\r\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem, Ertapenem.\r\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\r\nMeropenem-Varbobactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Imipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\r\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\r\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','0','0'),
(5,'Escherichia coli','Gram-negative,	aerobic, motile, flagellated, non-spore forming bacteria.  Predominant gram-negative component of normal human colonic flora. Seen with intra-abdominal and postoperative wound infections, and skin and soft tissues cellulitis, often in diabetics. Antibiotic resistance common, due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.\n','Ampicillin.\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCeftriaxone.\nCefazolin.\nTMP-SMX.',0,'NULL','Ciprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nMeropenem-Varbobactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.','Ceftolozane-tazobactam.\nPolymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B.\nCefiderocol.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\n','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\n','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem, Doripenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\n','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','0','0'),
(6,'Klebsiella oxytoca','Gram-negative, lactose fermenting aerobic bacilli. Normally found in the human gut flora, can cause nosocomial outbreaks, especially in compromised hosts.  It can infect skin and soft tissue post injury, surgery, or instrumentation. High levels of drug resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.','Amoxicillin/clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Ceftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.',0,'','Ciprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nMeropenem-varbobactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nPolymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\n','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTygecycline.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nCefiderocol.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','',''),
(7,'Klebsiella pneumoniae','Gram-negative, lactose fermenting aerobic bacilli. Normally found in the human gut flora. It can cause nosocomial outbreaks, especially in compromised hosts.  It can infect skin and soft tissue post injury, surgery, or instrumentation. High levels of drug resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.','Amoxicillin/clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCefazolin, Ceftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.',0,'NULL','Ciprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nMeropenem-varbobactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nTigecycline.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nPolymyxin B.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\n','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTygecycline.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nCefiderocol.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Imipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','0','0'),
(8,'Proteus mirabilis','Aerobic, Gram-negative, urease-splitting rod. It can cause nosocomial, vascular-catheter related infections, burn wound infections and surgical site infections. 	Intrinsically resistant to Polymyxins (Colistin, Polymyxin B).  High levels of drug resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\n',0,'NULL','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nCiprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nMeropenem-varbobactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam\nCefiderocol.\nTigecycline.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nTygecycline.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam\nCefiderocol.\nTigecycline.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','0','0'),
(9,'Proteus vulgaris','Aerobic, Gram-negative, urease-splitting rod. It can cause nosocomial, vascular-catheter related infections, burn wound infections and surgical site infections. 	Intrinsically resistant to Polymyxins (Colistin, Polymyxin B).  High levels of drug resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\n',0,'','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nCiprofloxacin,  Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nMeropenem-varbobactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam\nCefiderocol.\nTigecycline.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nTygecycline.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam\nCefiderocol.\nTigecycline.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin\nTigecycline.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.','','','','','','','','','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Amoxicillin-clavulanate, Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-Cilastatin,  Ertapenem.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nTigecycline.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','',''),
(10,'Pseudomonas aeruginosa','Gram-negative non-fermenting, motile bacillus. Usually a nosocomial, opportunistic pathogen. It can cause skin and soft tissue infections (neutropenia, cellulitis , folliculitis, abscesses) in immunocompromised hosts. Risk factors include immunosuppression, diabetes, skin burns, AIDS. Multiple pathways for ESBL and carbapenem resistance, including non-tested determinants such as OprD changes and drug efflux systems. Detected genoptype resistances due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases also common.','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCeftazidime, Cefepime.\nMeropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nTobramycin, Gentamicin.',0,'NULL','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nMeropenem-varbobactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nPlazomicin, Gentamicin, Tobramycin.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.\nPlazomicin, Gentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nCefiderocol.\nPolymyxin B.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nCefiderocol.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nPolymyxin B.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nPolymyxin B.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nPolymyxin B.','Meropenem, Imipenem-cilastatin.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nPolymyxin B.','','','','','','','','','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','0','0'),
(11,'Morganella morganii','Facultative anaerobic gram-negative rod. Environmentally ubiquitous, part of the normal colonic flora. It can cause surgical wound infections and soft tissue abscesses in compromised hosts. Risk factors include immune compromise, prolonged hospital stay and instrumentation use. Inherently resistant to penicillin, ampicillin, 1st & 2nd gen. cephalosporins, and macrolides. Additional antibiotic resistance seen due to expression of ampC, ESBLs, and Carbapenemases. Also commonly resistant to Fluoroquinolones, due to drug efflux systems, mutation genes as well as plasmid-mediated resistance ((QnrA, QnrB).','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nCefepime.\nMeropenem, Ertapenem.\nLevofloxacin.\nTobramycin, Gentamicin.',0,'NULL','Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nLevofloxacin.\nPlazomicin, Gentamicin, Tobramycin.\nMeropenem-varbobactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.','Meropenem-vaborbactam.\nCeftazidime-avibactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin-relebactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nCefiderocol.','Ceftazidime-avibactam + Aztreonam.\nCefiderocol.','Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nLevofloxacin.','Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nLevofloxacin.','Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nLevofloxacin.','Meropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nLevofloxacin.','','','','','','','','','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Ertapenem. ','Piperacillin-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Ertapenem.\nCeftolozane-tazobactam.\nMeropenem, Ertapenem. ','0','0'),
(12,'Bacteroides','Gram-negative, strict anaerobic bacilli. Normal components of intestinal, oral and vaginal flora. Common in skin and soft tissue infections due to bites, post-surgical, necrotizing fasciitis, and diabetic ulcers. Low rate of antibiotic resistance (<2%). Generally sensitive to metronidazole, carbapenems, beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations. ','Metronidazole.\nPiperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate.',0,'','Metronidazole.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.','Metronidazole.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTigecycline.','Metronidazole.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTigecycline.','Metronidazole.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTigecycline.','Metronidazole.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTigecycline.','Metronidazole.\nPolymyxin B, Colistin.\nTigecycline.','Metronidazole.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.','Metronidazole.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.','Metronidazole.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.','Metronidazole.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.','','','','','','','','','Metronidazole.\nPiperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate.','Metronidazole.\nPiperacillin-tazobactam.\nImipenem-cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate.','',''),
(13,'Kingella kingae','Gram-negative, facultative anaerobic coccobacillus. Major cause of musculoskeletal infections in younger children, with clusters in schools and daycare centers. Intrinsically resistant vancomycin, clindamycin, oxacillin, nafcillin. Expression of beta-lactamases common.','Ampicillin.\nAmpicillin-sublactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nTMP-SMX.\nCeftriaxone, Cefuroxime.\nErythromycin. \nGentamicin, Tobramycin.\nMeropenem.',0,'','','','','','','','Meropenem.\nErythromycin. \nAmpicillin-sublactam.\nCiprofloxacin, Levofloxacin.\nGentamicin, Tobramycin.','','','','','','','','','','','','','','',''),
(14,'Staphylococcus aureus','Aerobic, gram-positive cocci. Carried normally in anterior nares by about a third of the U.S. population. Carriers have a greater risk of subsequent infection. A common cause of skin and soft tissue infections, including folliculitis, cellulitis, furuncles, carbuncles, abscesses, impetigo, uncomplicated cellulitis, skin and soft tissue post-surgical infections, and necrotizing fasciitis. Most isolates (> 95%) are penicillin resistant. A large fraction (>50%) also test positive for mecA, which confers resistance to methicillin, oxacillin, nafcillin, and cephalosporins. Clindamycin susceptibility vary geographically, while vancomycin resistance remains rare.','Nafcillin, Oxacillin, Dicloxacillin.\nTMP-SMX.\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate.\nDoxycycline, Minocycline, Tigecycline.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nClindamycin.\nVancomycin, Linezolid, Daptomycin\nDoxycycline, Minocycline, Tigecycline.\nDalbavancin, Oritavancin.',1,'NULL','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Clindamycin.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nTMP/SMX.\nDoxycycline, Minocycline.\nLinezolid, Tedizolid.\nCeftaroline.\nTelavancin.','Clindamycin.\nVancomycin, Daptomycin.\nTMP/SMX.\nDoxycycline, Minocycline.\nLinezolid, Tedizolid.\nCeftaroline.\nTelavancin.','TMP/SMX.\nDoxycycline, Minocycline, Tigecycline.\nLinezolid, Tedizolid.\nCeftaroline.\nDalbavancin, Oritavancin.','TMP/SMX.\nDoxycycline, Minocycline, Tigecycline.\nLinezolid, Tedizolid.\nCeftaroline.\nDalbavancin, Oritavancin.','TMP/SMX.\nDoxycycline, Minocycline, Tigecycline.\nLinezolid, Tedizolid.\nCeftaroline.\nDalbavancin, Oritavancin.','','','0','0'),
(15,'Streptococcus pyogenes','Aerotolerant, Gram-positive, Group A streptococcus. It colonizes the pharynx or skin of  about 20% of school children and 3% of adults. It can cause skin and soft tissue infections, including cellulitis, myositis and  necrotizing fasciitis. About 10% of isolates are macrolide resistant. Also high rates of resistance to doxycycline and fluoroquinolones.','Penicillin V.\nClindamycin.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nVancomycin, Linezolid.',1,'NULL','','','','','','','','','','','Penicillin V.\nClindamycin.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nLinezolid.','Penicillin V.\nClindamycin.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nLinezolid.','Penicillin V.\nClindamycin.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nLinezolid.','Clindamycin.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nVancomycin, Linezolid.','Clindamycin.\nCephalexin, Cefazolin.\nCeftriaxone.\nTMP-SMX.\nVancomycin, Linezolid.','','','','','','0','0'),
(16,'Enterococcus faecalis','Facultative anaerobic, gram-positive bacteria. Found in soil, water, food. Significant component of normal colonic flora; also present in oropharyngeal and vaginal secretions. A common cause of skin and soft tissue infections, surgical wounds infections, decubitus ulcers, and diabetic foot infections.  Intrinsically less susceptible to TMP/SMX, and aminoglycosides. Increasingly resistant  to Vancomycin, due to plasmid-mediated resistance mechanism (VRE).','Penicillin V, Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. \n',1,'NULL','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','','','','','','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Vancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin V, Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin V, Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin V, Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','','','Linezolid.\nTigecycline.\nOritavancin.\n\n','Linezolid.\nTigecycline.\nOritavancin.\n\n','Linezolid.\nTigecycline.\nOritavancin.\n\n','Penicillin V, Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','Penicillin V, Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nAmpicillin-Sulbactam.\nTigecycline. ','0','0'),
(17,'Enterococcus faecium','Facultative anaerobic, gram-positive bacteria. Found in soil, water, food. Significant component of normal colonic flora; also present in oropharyngeal and vaginal secretions. A common cause of skin and soft tissue infections, surgical wounds infections, decubitus ulcers, and diabetic foot infections.  Intrinsically less susceptible to TMP/SMX, beta-lactams, aminoglycosides. Increasingly resistant  to Vancomycin, due to plasmid-mediated resistance mechanism (VRE).','Penicillin G, Ampicillin.\nVancomycin.\nTigecycline. ',1,'','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','Linezolid.\nTigecycline.\nOritavancin.\nCeftriaxone.\nCeftaroline.','Linezolid.\nTigecycline.\nOritavancin.\nCeftriaxone.\nCeftaroline.','Linezolid.\nTigecycline.\nOritavancin.\nCeftriaxone.\nCeftaroline.','','','',''),
(18,'Clostridium novyi','Gram-positive, obligate anaerobic bacteria. A toxin producer. Normally reside in the environment or the gastrointestinal tract of humans and animals. Found in soft tissue infections due to traumatic wound or surgery: gas gangrene, crepitant cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis.  About 5% of clostridia are clindamycin-resistant.','Penicillin G + Clindamycin.\nVancomycin.\nChloramphenicol.\nPiperacillin-tazobactam.\nCeftriaxone + Clindamycin.\nCefepime + Clindamycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.',1,'','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','',''),
(19,'Clostridium perfringens','Gram-positive, obligate anaerobic bacteria. A toxin producer. Normally reside in the environment or the gastrointestinal tract of humans and animals. Found in soft tissue infections due to traumatic wound or surgery: gas gangrene, crepitant cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis.  About 5% of clostridia are clindamycin-resistant.','Penicillin G + Clindamycin.\nVancomycin.\nChloramphenicol.\nPiperacillin-tazobactam.\nCeftriaxone + Clindamycin.\nCefepime + Clindamycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.',1,'','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','',''),
(20,'Clostridium septicum','Gram-positive, obligate anaerobic bacteria. A toxin producer. Normally reside in the environment or the gastrointestinal tract of humans and animals. Found in soft tissue infections due to traumatic wound or surgery: gas gangrene, crepitant cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis.  About 5% of clostridia are clindamycin-resistant.','Penicillin G + Clindamycin.\nVancomycin.\nChloramphenicol.\nPiperacillin-tazobactam.\nCeftriaxone + Clindamycin.\nCefepime + Clindamycin.\nImipenem-Cilastatin, Meropenem,  Ertapenem.',1,'','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','','');
